C(630,623,'For a smooth proper curve in characteristicp, Cartier on differential forms is the dual of Frobenius on H1(O), compatible with residue/Serre duality. In top degree it is the duality trace, not the zero field trace of a purely inseparable extension.','proof sketched','B','R-Q09/11: Cartier’s original applications and the explicit duality formulation in Mazur–Messing I§4 match the proper smooth setting. Relative base-change assumptions are retained.',['RCartier1957','RMazurMessing1974'],computation='In a p-basis, the coefficient of x^(p−1)dx is selected by Cartier; the ordinary trace of the same inseparable degreep extension is zero. These two maps therefore cannot be identified.')
C(631,623,'On the Picard scheme of a smooth proper family where Pic0 is an abelian scheme, pullback by relative Frobenius on the curve corresponds to Verschiebung on Pic0; its cotangent map is Cartier under the duality identifications.','proof sketched','B','R-Q11: Mazur–Messing I§4 identifies de Rham cohomology with the Lie algebra of the universal vector extension. The smooth Picard hypotheses in the manuscript matter; no assertion about an arbitrary singular Picard functor is inferred.',['RMazurMessing1974'],confidence='medium')
I(624,93,[183,184],K(4),'reading question','C(f dg)','The pairing on Q=F*O/O is perfect, but its displayed index and determinant normalization have errors under the literal transcription.','Separate perfectness, determinant isomorphism, and the choice of square-root normalization; a sign error in the third does not refute the first.',check='understates',reason='The explicit p-basis matrix was recomputed. Its nonzero anti-diagonal has i+j=p, and p=5 gives determinant−1.')
C(632,624,'For a smooth curve in characteristicp, φ(f,g)=C(f dg) induces a perfect pairing Q×Q→ω_X′, alternating when p is odd; for p=2, Q is an invertible sheaf with Q²≅ω_X′.','proved there','B','R-Q12: Raynaud1982 §4.1 pp118–119 explicitly gives this pairing and the characteristic2 theta characteristic; its Theorem4.1.1 adds a theta-divisor result, which is not claimed on these pages.',['RRaynaud1982'],computation='root_computations.py constructs the anti-diagonal matrix j·[i+j=p] for p2–19; root_computation_notes.md proves well-definedness, symmetry type and determinant nonvanishing.',date='Raynaud received19March1981, revised15June1981; published1982. Manuscript date unknown.')
C(633,624,'The assertion that the displayed pairing matrix always has determinant+1 is false, although its determinant is always nonzero.','false as stated','C','R-Q12: Raynaud’s perfect-pairing result survives the corrected normalization. The explicit p=5 counterexample refines the source to C-SA04-634.',['RRaynaud1982'],computation='For basis x,x²,x³,x4 at p=5, the matrix has anti-diagonal4,3,2,1 and determinant4=−1, not1. The source assumes only a smooth curve/p-basis, satisfied by A1/F5.')
C(634,624,'For odd p the alternating pairing yields a canonical Pfaffian isomorphism detQ→ω^((p−1)/2); its square must be compared with the determinant isomorphism with the correct sign convention.','proof sketched','B','R-Q12: Raynaud’s self-duality agrees with the determinant-line construction. The exact basis normalization in the transcription needs the sign correction proved here.',['RRaynaud1982'],computation='detφ=(-1)^((p−1)(p−2)/2)(p−1)!; for oddp this is(-1)^((p−1)/2+1). Pfaffian squaring gives the canonical normalized determinant relation.')
I(625,93,[184,185],K(4),'conjecture','corps imparfait','The page asserts the existence of pointless rational curves over imperfect characteristic2 fields and asks for a higher-dimensional analogue of the pairing construction.','The universal field quantifier is distinguished from the valid existence of some anisotropic conics; the source may have a reading problem at that quantifier.')
C(635,625,'The universal claim that every imperfect characteristic2 field admits a smooth conic without a rational point is false.','false as stated','C','R-Q12/13: the smooth-point lemma Stacks056U gives the precise obstruction to this universal formulation; Raynaud’s pairing theorem does not imply anisotropic conics over every field. Refined statement C-SA04-636.',['RStacks056U','RRaynaud1982'],computation='Take k=F2(t)^sep. It remains imperfect since t has no square root in a separable extension. Every nonempty smooth finite-type k-scheme has a closed point with finite separable residue field, hence a k-point. In particular every smooth conic does. All field and characteristic hypotheses are met.')
C(636,625,'Over a separably closed field every nonempty smooth finite-type scheme has a rational point; therefore the conic construction must impose a further field/Brauer condition rather than mere imperfection.','stated without proof','E','R-Q13: Stacks Lemma33.25.6, tag056U, proves density of closed points with finite separable residue field on smooth schemes. This is a correction supplied by the present audit, not a claim attributed to the manuscript.',['RStacks056U'],computation='The counterexample and implication are proved in root_computation_notes.md.',words='Audit refinement of the universal field assertion; not a manuscript quotation.')
I(626,93,[186],K(4),'withdrawn in the same text','dualité','A Hodge/conjugate-filtration duality sketch contains cancellations and revised hypotheses.','Only the surviving proper smooth duality formulation is retained; cancelled assertions are not presented as the final theorem.')
C(637,626,'For a smooth proper family with the required de Rham duality and locally free base-change hypotheses, the Hodge and conjugate filtrations have the expected orthogonality under the cup-product pairing. The broader cancelled formulation is withdrawn.','corrected by himself later','C','R-Q09/11: Cartier and Mazur–Messing supply the smooth duality setting. The page’s cancellations and unverified base-change assumptions block an unconditional relative statement.',['RCartier1957','RMazurMessing1974'],confidence='medium')
I(627,93,[189,190,191,192,193],K(4),'reading question','intégrable','Here the author defines integrable by étale local triviality and discusses monodromy representations of stratified bundles.','This local definition is stronger than curvaturezero. Continuous representations on discrete finite-dimensional fibres have finite image when the relevant group is profinite.',check='understates',reason='The special definition on scan189 was read before interpreting later monodromy claims; standard modern flatness alone cannot be substituted.')
C(638,627,'Étale-locally trivial stratified bundles are classified by the appropriate continuous monodromy representations with their descent data; this is not a classification of all integrable algebraic connections.','proof sketched','E','R-Q14 searched SGA1-style finite étale descent and Deligne1970’s connection/monodromy formalism. The comparison is restricted to the source’s étale-trivial category, and no equality with all flat bundles is claimed.',['BDeligne1970'],computation='A trivializing étale cover supplies transition matrices locally constant for the stratification; the cocycle condition is precisely descent. This argument only proves the indicated subcategory statement.')
I(628,93,[192,193],K(4),'other','rang 1','The rank-one subgroup of étale-trivial objects is described by torsion line bundles and logarithmic units.','In characteristicp, prime-to-p torsion is the immediate Kummer finite-étale case; in characteristiczero all finite orders are invertible.')
C(639,628,'For the Kummer finite-étale rank-one situation, torsion line bundles of order invertible on the base carry the corresponding finite-monodromy stratified structures; arbitrary flat line bundles need not have finite monodromy.','proof sketched','E','R-Q14/15: Deligne’s monodromy formalism and Bost’s later arithmetic theorem keep the finite-monodromy condition separate. The statement here is the elementary Kummer case, not a classification over an unspecified singular base.',['BDeligne1970','RBost2001'],computation='Trivializing L^n and adjoining an nth root of its transition data gives a finite étale cover when n is invertible; descent gives the connection/stratification.')
C(640,628,'Over an algebraically closed characteristiczero field, for a connected reduced finite-type scheme with the relevant unit finiteness, O(X)* / k*≅Z^r and rational multiples of logarithmic derivatives yield a (Q/Z)^r contribution modulo integral logarithmic derivatives.','proof sketched','E','R-Q14: Stacks04L6 verifies the unit-group finiteness under its exact hypotheses. The quotient calculation is then elementary; torsion and disconnected-base variants are not suppressed.',['RStacks04L6'],computation='Choose a Z-basis of the free unit quotient; tensor with Q and divide by the lattice. Multiplicative constants have zero dlog, giving the stated contribution.')
I(629,93,[194,195,196],K(5),'conjecture','p-courbure','The arithmetic converse from vanishing reductions of p-curvature to finite monodromy is posed, with a separate rank-one reduction.','Use a connection defined over a finitely generated characteristiczero ring and good reductions at almost all primes; one prime or infinitely many primes is a different assertion.')
C(641,629,'A characteristiczero algebraic integrable connection whose p-curvatures vanish for almost all good residue characteristics is conjectured to have finite monodromy, equivalently to become trivial after a finite étale cover in the usual smooth setting.','open on the pages','C','R-Q15: Katz1972 and1982 formulate and develop the conjecture. Lam–Litt2026 still states the general conjecture and proves a different isomonodromy/nonabelian result; Patel–Shankar–Whang settle their rank-two generic-curve case. Neither is a proof of the unrestricted statement.',['RKatz1972','RKatz1982','RPSW2021','RLamLitt2026'],confidence='high',present='General conjecture remains open in the checked November2026 primary literature; the named special cases have strictly narrower hypotheses.',date='Katz publication1972; Katz1982 received19September1981; PSW preprint12November2018/published2021; Lam–Litt preprint12January2026.')
C(642,629,'For a flat line bundle over a smooth connected variety over a number field, almost-everywhere zero p-curvature implies finite étale triviality.','open on the pages','B','R-Q15: Bost2001 Theorem2.9 proves the equivalence for principal G-bundles when G0 is solvable, hence G=Gm. Earlier work is discussed there; an exact first-priority claim for Honda is not made without opening the original.',['RBost2001'],present='The number-field rank-one case is established. The manuscript’s individual torsion/logarithm reduction steps still require their own hypotheses.',date='Bost published2001; earlier first circulation/priority not established by this audit.')
I(630,93,[196,197],K(5),'conjecture','extension universelle','The rank-one arithmetic problem is reformulated in the universal vector extension and in terms of algebraic subgroups detected after reduction.','The exact arithmetic model, integral section and density quantifiers must be retained; a related Lie-algebra theorem is not automatically the same section statement.')
C(643,630,'The universal-vector-extension reformulation relates arithmetic p-curvature conditions to algebraicity of a subgroup/Lie direction, but the strongest section-specialization claim written here is not proved by that reformulation alone.','open on the pages','C','R-Q15/16: Bost2001 Theorem2.3 proves algebraicity of a Lie subalgebra detected by restricted reductions over number fields; Mazur–Messing identifies the universal extension. The manuscript’s section and specialization quantifiers require an additional comparison, which these citations do not supply.',['RBost2001','RMazurMessing1974'],confidence='medium')
I(631,93,[198],K(5),'conjecture','Verschiebung','A specialization question uses divisibility by Verschiebung of sections in residue characteristics.','Divisibility must mean a lift over the stated residue field (or another explicitly fixed field). Fppf-local divisibility by an isogeny would be automatic and would destroy the arithmetic content.')
C(644,631,'The proposed conclusion from Verschiebung divisibility of reductions of a section requires specified rational lifts and a quantified set of primes; it is not implied merely by fppf surjectivity of Verschiebung.','open on the pages','C','R-Q16 searched universal vector extension, Verschiebung-divisibility and specialization formulations, with backward references from Bost and Mazur–Messing. Their theorems settle related Lie and extension structures, but no exact theorem with the page’s section quantifiers was identified.',['RBost2001','RMazurMessing1974'],confidence='medium',present='The rational-lift version is a precise remaining comparison problem; the fppf reading is vacuous.')
I(632,93,[199,200,201,202,203],K(5),'other','Cartier itéré','Iterates of Cartier and their stable image are compared with Frobenius and the unit-root part.','A semilinear operator must be linearized with the appropriate base twist before eigenvalues or ranks are compared.')
C(645,632,'On finite-dimensional cohomology over a perfect field, iterated Cartier/Frobenius yields stable-image and nilpotent parts; relative base-change assertions need extra flatness.','proof sketched','B','R-Q09/11/17: Cartier’s local calculation and the de Rham/universal-extension comparison in Mazur–Messing give the standard setting; the source’s relative qualifications are preserved.',['RCartier1957','RMazurMessing1974'],computation='For a semilinear endomorphism over a perfect field, descending image dimensions stabilize; on the stable image the map is bijective and the quotient is nilpotent. This elementary argument does not prove commutation with arbitrary base change.')
C(646,632,'For a smooth proper curve over a finite field, the degree of the unit-root factor corresponds to geometric p-rank, whereas rational p-torsion depends on the fixed space of arithmetic Frobenius.','proof sketched','B','R-Q17: Milne’s abelian-variety point-count formulas and the Artin–Schreier genus/p-rank formulas supply a checked test case. The distinction between stable rank and rational fixed vectors is essential.',['RMilne2008','RAS2025'],computation='The genus-two example in C-SA04-647 has geometric p-rank2 (three Artin–Schreier poles) but rational2-torsion dimension1.')
I(633,93,[204],K(5),'unproved claim','nb des valeurs propres','The last numerical comparison identifies a Cartier fixed-space dimension/rational p-torsion dimension with a count of eigenvalues congruent1.','The refutation concerns algebraic multiplicity. If the ink/terminology instead means geometric multiplicity, C-SA04-648 is the correct reading.',check='understates',reason='No semisimplicity hypothesis appears in K93-05 line245. A smooth genus-two curve over F2 separates the two multiplicities.')
C(647,633,'Counting the algebraic multiplicity of eigenvalue1 modulo p does not in general give dim_Fp J(k)[p], even for a smooth proper geometrically connected curve over k=Fp.','false as stated','C','R-Q17: the checked point-count and Artin–Schreier formulas enable an explicit counterexample; the source should be refined to C-SA04-648. No general semisimplicity theorem repairs the missing hypothesis.',['RMilne2008','RAS2025'],computation='C/F2: z²+(x²+x+1)z=x5+x3+x²+1. Smooth projective genus2; N1..4=3,7,9,31; P(T)=1+T²+4T4; #J(F2)=6. Hence dimJ(F2)[2]=1 while P mod2=(1+T)² has multiplicity2. root_computations.py and c_review_genus2.py use independent counting methods; all hypotheses are proved in root_computation_notes.md.')
C(648,633,'For an abelian variety over a finite field, rational p-torsion is the Frobenius-fixed subspace of its geometric étale p-torsion. The eigenvalue1 algebraic multiplicity gives that dimension only with semisimplicity at1.','stated without proof','E','R-Q17: this is the descent/fixed-point interpretation, supported by the explicit counterexample and the standard finite-field framework. It is an audit refinement, not a new assertion quoted from the manuscript.',['RMilne2008'],computation='A rational geometric torsion point is exactly a Galois-fixed point; over a finite field the Galois group is generated topologically by Frobenius. A Jordan block at1 has one fixed vector but contributes its full size to algebraic multiplicity.',words='Audit refinement of K93-05 line245; not a manuscript quotation.')
