C(649,634,'When Pic0_X/S is an abelian scheme and the indicated differential/Albanese comparison hypotheses hold, the identity component of the Picard functor with integrable connections is its universal vector extension; its Lie algebra identifies with H1_dR.','proof sketched','B','R-Q11: Mazur–Messing I(2.6.7), I(3.2.3), I(4.1.4),(4.1.7) develops the rigidified-extension, connection and de Rham identifications. The manuscript’s general Picard representability assumptions remain explicit.',['RMazurMessing1974'],date='Published1974; introduction attributes the crystalline programme to Grothendieck’s letter to Tate, without dating these manuscript sheets.')
C(650,634,'For smooth X/S in characteristicp, Cartier descent identifies rank-one zero-p-curvature connections with Pic(X′/S); nilpotent rank-one p-curvature corresponds, on nilpotent test schemes under the stated finiteness conditions, to the formal completion along that zero locus.','proof sketched','B','R-Q09/11/18: Cartier descent supplies the zero locus; Mazur–Messing gives its universal-extension setting. The completion assertion is checked directly from nilpotence of the scalar p-curvature coordinates, and requires the formal/test-scheme convention.',['RCartier1957','RMazurMessing1974'],computation='For a line bundle End(L)=O. Scalar p-curvature is nilpotent iff it vanishes after reduction; on a noetherian test scheme the finitely generated nil ideal is nilpotent. This is precisely factorization through some infinitesimal neighborhood of the zero locus. Scan212’s kernel label Pcip conflicts with the definition Pcin.')
I(635,93,[214,215,216,217],K(6),'self-flagged step','c^1(L)','The obstruction to an integrable line connection is a truncated-de-Rham hypercohomology class; the Pic0-vanishing hope and a secondary obstruction are separated.','Do not replace the truncated obstruction by its image in full de Rham cohomology, and retain the secondary class’s indeterminacy.')
C(651,635,'Transition dlog functions define the obstruction to a line-bundle connection in H1(Ω¹), and to an integrable connection in hypercohomology of Ω≥1; choosing a first lift leaves a secondary curvature obstruction modulo the indicated changes of local connection.','proof sketched','B','R-Q06/11/18: Deligne’s connection formalism and Mazur–Messing’s Picard interpretation match this obstruction sequence. The page’s unrestricted hope that every Pic0 class has vanishing obstruction is not proved merely by the abelian-scheme special case.',['BDeligne1970','RMazurMessing1974'],computation='Čech transition functions gij obey dlog(gij)+dlog(gjk)=dlog(gik); local one-forms differ by dlog(gij), and their exterior derivatives glue. Changing them by a global one-form changes curvature by its differential, giving the stated indeterminacy.')
I(636,93,[217],K(6),'self-flagged step','conoyau','An additive p-polynomial map and its cokernel are used in the Picard-with-connection analysis.','A flat kernel alone does not establish that the cokernel is a vector bundle; representability, local freeness and rank conditions must be checked separately.')
C(652,636,'The additive-polynomial cokernel construction on scan217 needs additional representability/local-freeness hypotheses before it can be called a vector bundle.','open on the pages','C','R-Q18 searched additive p-polynomial cokernels, Picard connections and the corresponding universal extensions. Mazur–Messing treats the structured abelian case; no source was found proving the unrestricted implication from the single flatness condition written here.',['RMazurMessing1974'],confidence='medium',present='A conditional construction, with the missing cokernel hypotheses isolated; no unsupported counterexample to the precise manuscript data is asserted.')
